    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "related: #40795, #38760\n\n## Problem\n\nThe [tar plugin](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/master/runtime/autoload/tar.vim) should be moved to lua like the zip plugin, eventually seeking a unified vim.archive interface (maybe).\n\nThis issue tracks the full tar plugin surface and related decisions to be tackled in a few PRs.\n\n## Existing behavior\n\nThe tar plugin provides the following functionality:\n\n- Detect tar-like files by extension and open them as archive listings.\n- Detect `tarfile:{archive}::{member}` buffers and read/write archive members.\n- Render a browser buffer showing archive members, maximized unless `g:tar_nomax`.\n- Open a selected member from the browser with `<CR>` or `<LeftMouse>`.\n- Write changes from a member buffer back into the archive with `tar -uf`.\n- Extract the selected member to the current directory with `x`.\n- Diff an archived member against its on-disk counterpart with `:TarDiff`.\n- Decompress `gz`, `bz2`, `bz3`, `xz`, `lzma`, `zst`, `lz4`, and `Z` archives by piping through an external tool before tar.\n- Support user-configurable tar, browse, read, write, delete, and extract commands.\n- Preserve leading `./` and `/` member paths, path traversal checks, and shell quoting behaviors.\n\n## Progress Tracker\n\n- [ ] Add an opt-in `tar.lua` browser and member reader.\n\n  The first implementation is private and read-only, uses `nvim.dir`, recognizes the complete tar extension set, and opens members through `nvim-tar://{archive}/{member}` buffers. Ship it as the default, with the old legacy being opt-in.\n\n- [ ] Stabilize the minimum browser surface.\n\n  Settle extension dispatch, false-positive fallback, archive-directory navigation, syntax, member identity, and read-only buffer behavior before expanding the plugin.\n\n- [ ] Decide the decompression strategy.\n\n  Either dispatch per extension to an external decompressor as the legacy plugin does, or delegate to tar's own auto-detection.\n\n- [ ] Add `:checkhealth nvim.tar`.\n\n- [ ] Decide and port extraction.\n\n  Add an `x` action. The zip plugin settled this already and tar should not diverge without cause.\n\n- [ ] Decide member writeback (probably not).\n\n- [ ] Decide platform and remote behavior.\n\n  Handle `:TarDiff` and `g:tar_*` configuration surface (hopefully all of it can\n  be removed).\n",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Perhaps instead of creating a new `tar.lua` plugin, you can instead expand `zip.lua` into a generalized `archive.lua` which supports both zip and tar archives? (since that's the end goal post-`vim.archive`) This will also give us a better idea of what type of API a future `vim.archive` should provide.",
